# coupling-viewmodel.xrs - is the weapon/hand viewmodel GLUED TO THE VIEW, or
# left behind in the world when you turn your head?
#
# This is a difference-of-differences test, and it is the reason captures beat
# eyeballing: turn the head by a known amount and compare the SAME screen region
# before and after.
#
#   - A view-locked viewmodel stays in the same place on screen. The region where
#     the gun sits barely changes; the world behind it changes a lot.
#   - A world-locked viewmodel slides across the screen (or leaves frame) with
#     exactly the same magnitude as the world.
#
# How to read it, with img-diff's grid so you can see WHERE it changed:
#
#   .\tools\img-diff.ps1 -A vm_yaw00_left.png -B vm_yaw20_left.png -Grid 16
#
# The viewmodel occupies the lower-right of the frame in BS1. If that block of
# the coverage map is quiet while the rest is loud, the viewmodel is following
# the head, which is correct. If the viewmodel block is as loud as the world, it
# is world-locked - the bug this sequence exists to catch.
#
# Then repeat with TRANSLATION rather than rotation (below): a view-locked
# viewmodel is also immune to head TRANSLATION, and translation is the case that
# usually exposes a viewmodel parented to the wrong space.
# Stereo armed here, not left to the caller - see coupling-hand.xrs.
reset
@mod vrstereo on
@mod vrhands on
@frames 60
@assert projectionViews eq 2
@shot vm_yaw00
head rot 20 0 0
@frames 20
@shot vm_yaw20
head rot 0 0 0
@frames 20
# Strafe half a metre without turning. The world must shift; a correctly
# parented viewmodel must not.
head pos 0.5 1.6 0
@frames 20
@shot vm_strafe
# And lean in, which changes parallax on everything world-locked.
head pos 0 1.6 -0.5
@frames 20
@shot vm_lean
head pos 0 1.6 0
@frames 10
